  <section class="section" id="s1">
    <h2>1. What is Cloud Forensics?</h2>
    <p class="lead">Cloud forensics is the discipline of applying traditional digital forensic science to cloud computing environments — identifying, collecting, preserving, analyzing, and presenting digital evidence stored across distributed, virtualized systems.</p>

    <p>When a cybercrime occurs — whether it's a data breach, insider threat, ransomware attack, or unauthorized access — investigators need to find out <em>what happened, when, where, by whom, and how</em>. In the physical world, this meant pulling hard drives from servers and analyzing them. In cloud computing, evidence is scattered across virtual machines, storage buckets, logs, and API calls — often spanning multiple countries and cloud providers simultaneously.</p>

    <div class="def-box">
      <p><strong>Cloud Forensics</strong> is the application of scientific methods to identify, collect, preserve, examine, and present digital evidence from cloud computing environments in a manner that is legally admissible and forensically sound. It bridges computer forensics, incident response, and cloud security engineering.</p>
    </div>

    <p>Think of a traditional crime scene: investigators arrive, photograph everything, bag evidence, and preserve chain of custody. Cloud forensics does the same — except the "crime scene" is spread across thousands of virtual servers, and you may not physically own or control the hardware where your data lives.</p>

    <h3>Why Cloud Forensics is Different</h3>
    <p>Traditional forensics relies on physical access to hardware. Cloud forensics must work around several fundamental differences:</p>

    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>Multi-tenancy:</strong> Multiple organizations share the same physical infrastructure, making physical evidence isolation impossible</li>
      <li><strong>Jurisdictional complexity:</strong> Data may sit in servers across 20+ countries simultaneously, each governed by different legal frameworks</li>
      <li><strong>Ephemeral resources:</strong> Virtual machines can be spun up and destroyed in seconds, deleting evidence before an investigator arrives</li>
      <li><strong>Limited physical access:</strong> Investigators cannot simply "pull a hard drive" — they depend entirely on logs and APIs provided by the cloud provider</li>
      <li><strong>Dynamic data:</strong> Cloud systems are constantly moving, replicating, and altering data, making a consistent forensic snapshot difficult</li>
      <li><strong>Encryption challenges:</strong> Cloud data is often encrypted at rest and in transit, requiring cooperation from key holders</li>
    </ul>

