  <section class="section" id="s2">
    <h2>2. A Brief History</h2>
    <p class="lead">Cloud forensics emerged from the collision of two worlds: the well-established discipline of digital forensics (born in the 1980s) and the explosive growth of cloud computing (2006 onward).</p>

    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-year">1984</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          <div class="tl-title">Digital Forensics is Born</div>
          <div class="tl-text">The FBI establishes the first computer forensics unit. Early work focuses on recovering deleted files from floppy disks and analyzing mainframe logs.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-year">1999</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          <div class="tl-title">First Forensic Standards</div>
          <div class="tl-text">The ACPO (Association of Chief Police Officers) publishes the first widely adopted guidelines for computer-based evidence. Chain of custody principles formalized.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-year">2006</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          <div class="tl-title">Amazon Web Services Launches</div>
          <div class="tl-text">AWS launches S3 and EC2, inaugurating the modern cloud era. Data begins migrating off physical premises at unprecedented scale, fundamentally disrupting forensic workflows.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-year">2010</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          <div class="tl-title">NIST Defines Cloud Computing</div>
          <div class="tl-text">NIST Special Publication 800-145 formally defines cloud computing with the five essential characteristics, three service models, and four deployment models still used today.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-year">2013</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          <div class="tl-title">NIST Cloud Forensics Taxonomy</div>
          <div class="tl-text">NIST publishes SP 800-61r2 and begins work on cloud forensic challenges taxonomy. Cloud forensics recognized as a distinct scientific discipline requiring new frameworks.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-year">2017</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          <div class="tl-title">GDPR &amp; Cross-Border Evidence Laws</div>
          <div class="tl-text">Europe's General Data Protection Regulation creates sweeping new rules about where data can be stored and accessed, directly impacting cloud forensic investigations across borders.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tl-item">
        <div class="tl-year">2024+</div>
        <div class="tl-body">
          <div class="tl-title">AI-Assisted Cloud Forensics</div>
          <div class="tl-text">Machine learning tools now assist in log analysis, anomaly detection, and evidence correlation across petabytes of cloud data. Container and serverless forensics emerge as new frontiers.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

