    <h3>Deep Dive: The Most Critical Risks</h3>

    <h4>Risk 1 — Misconfiguration</h4>
    <p>Misconfiguration is the single largest source of cloud security incidents. A misconfigured S3 bucket left publicly readable, an overly permissive IAM policy, or an open security group can expose millions of records instantly. The Capital One breach of 2019 exposed over 100 million customer records due to a misconfigured AWS Web Application Firewall — the attacker exploited a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerability amplified by excessive IAM permissions.</p>

    <div class="analogy">
      <div class="analogy-label">Analogy</div>
      <p>Misconfiguration is like leaving your house key under the doormat and then being surprised when someone uses it. The lock (encryption/security service) is perfectly functional — but the <em>configuration</em> (where you put the key) made it useless.</p>
    </div>

    <h4>Risk 2 — Insufficient Identity and Access Management (IAM)</h4>
    <p>In cloud environments, identity IS the new perimeter. When IAM is weak — through reused passwords, missing multi-factor authentication, over-privileged roles, or unrotated access keys — attackers can impersonate legitimate users and blend in with normal activity, making forensic attribution extremely difficult.</p>

    <h4>Risk 3 — Data Volatility and Evidence Destruction</h4>
    <p>Cloud resources are designed to be ephemeral by nature. Auto-scaling groups spin up and terminate VMs automatically. Containers live for minutes. Serverless functions execute for milliseconds. This is excellent for cost efficiency but catastrophic for forensic investigation — evidence is routinely destroyed before anyone realizes an incident has occurred.</p>

    <div class="danger-callout">
      <p><strong>⚠ Critical Alert:</strong> In AWS environments, default CloudTrail logging retains data for only 90 days. EC2 instance memory is completely unrecoverable once a VM is terminated. Without proactive forensic readiness measures, critical evidence is permanently lost within days of an incident.</p>
    </div>

    <h4>Risk 4 — Multi-Tenancy and Cross-Contamination</h4>
    <p>When your organization's virtual machines run on the same physical hardware as another company's VMs (standard in public cloud), there exists a theoretical risk of data leakage across tenant boundaries — known as a "VM escape" or "side-channel attack." While rare, these events create complex forensic situations where the attacker's evidence may sit in a neighboring tenant's allocation.</p>

    <h4>Risk 5 — Jurisdictional Complexity</h4>
    <p>When you store data in AWS us-east-1, your data may physically reside in Virginia, USA — but AWS replicates it for redundancy across multiple availability zones. If you store in eu-west-1, that's Ireland. Cross-border forensic requests require Mutual Legal Assistance Treaties (MLATs), which can take 6–18 months, by which time the cloud evidence may be long gone.</p>

    <div class="cards-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🔑</span>
        <h4>Credential Theft</h4>
        <p>Stolen API keys or tokens grant cloud-native access with no need to break encryption or bypass perimeters.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">👻</span>
        <h4>Shadow IT</h4>
        <p>Employees creating unauthorized cloud resources outside IT control, leaving forensic blind spots with no logs or audit trails.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🔗</span>
        <h4>Supply Chain Attacks</h4>
        <p>Compromising a cloud-hosted software dependency (like a library in npm or PyPI) to attack downstream customers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">📡</span>
        <h4>Data Exfiltration</h4>
        <p>Attackers using legitimate cloud APIs (S3 sync, Cloud Storage transfers) to steal data, making exfiltration look like normal operations.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

