    <h3>Why This Model Matters for Forensics</h3>
    <p>When a security incident occurs, the shared responsibility model determines who can access what evidence. A cloud provider will have logs of their infrastructure layer (network traffic, hypervisor events, data center access logs) — but they typically will <em>not</em> hand these over without a valid legal subpoena. Meanwhile, the customer controls application logs, IAM audit trails, and data access logs — but may not have configured them properly.</p>

    <p>The forensic investigator must understand this division intimately because it determines:</p>
    <ol class="styled">
      <li>What evidence you can access immediately vs. what requires legal process</li>
      <li>What evidence you are legally responsible for preserving during litigation</li>
      <li>Where the attacker operated and which party's logs would contain their traces</li>
      <li>What data you can lawfully share with law enforcement without breaching privacy laws</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <h4>Forensic Readiness Principle</h4>
      <p>Organizations should establish forensic readiness <em>before</em> an incident occurs. This means enabling CloudTrail/Stackdriver/Azure Monitor logging from day one, setting log retention to at least 12 months, encrypting logs and storing them in a separate, immutable account, and establishing legal agreements with your cloud provider for expedited evidence access during incidents.</p>
    </div>
