    <h3>IaaS — Infrastructure as a Service</h3>
    <p>In IaaS (AWS EC2, Google Compute Engine, Azure VMs), the customer manages everything from the OS upward. This gives forensic investigators the greatest access — they can take memory snapshots, analyze disk images, review OS logs, and configure network monitoring. However, this also means the customer bears the greatest forensic responsibility. If you didn't configure CloudTrail, VPC Flow Logs, or CloudWatch, that evidence simply doesn't exist.</p>

    <h3>PaaS — Platform as a Service</h3>
    <p>In PaaS (AWS Elastic Beanstalk, Google App Engine, Heroku), the provider manages the OS and runtime. Forensic investigators lose direct OS access but retain application-level logging. Evidence collection is more abstracted — you're working with application metrics and platform audit logs rather than raw system calls and kernel events. Container forensics (Docker, Kubernetes) is a rapidly growing PaaS forensic domain.</p>

    <h3>SaaS — Software as a Service</h3>
    <p>In SaaS (Microsoft 365, Salesforce, Google Workspace), the provider controls almost everything. Forensic investigation is almost entirely limited to application-layer audit logs — who logged in, what files were accessed, what was deleted. The Microsoft 365 Unified Audit Log, for example, records over 140 types of events and is the primary forensic evidence source for Office 365 incidents. Getting OS-level or network-level evidence requires a formal legal request to Microsoft.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-icon">💡</span>
      <p><strong>Key Insight:</strong> As you move from IaaS → PaaS → SaaS, forensic investigators gain <em>convenience</em> (the provider manages more) but lose <em>forensic depth</em> (access to lower-level evidence). The best forensic posture combines all three: SaaS for productivity, IaaS for forensic-sensitive workloads where evidence control matters.</p>
    </div>
