    <h3>Phase 1: Identification</h3>
    <p>The first task is scoping: what cloud resources are involved? What timeframe? What accounts, regions, and services are in scope? Cloud resources must be identified using cloud-native inventory tools (AWS Config, Azure Resource Graph, GCP Asset Inventory). Crucially, alerts and monitoring systems must be configured to detect anomalies before they are manually discovered — because in cloud environments, attackers often dwell undetected for 200+ days on average.</p>

    <h3>Phase 2: Preservation</h3>
    <p>This is where cloud forensics diverges most sharply from traditional forensics. You cannot simply "seize" a cloud server. Instead, investigators must:</p>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li>Create EBS snapshots (AWS), disk images (Azure), or persistent disk snapshots (GCP) of running VMs</li>
      <li>Export memory dumps using VM introspection APIs before the instance is terminated</li>
      <li>Enable and export CloudTrail / Stackdriver / Azure Monitor logs immediately</li>
      <li>Isolate compromised instances by modifying security group rules (not terminating them)</li>
      <li>Hash all collected artifacts immediately using SHA-256 to establish integrity</li>
      <li>Document timestamps in UTC with timezone notation to avoid ambiguity</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-icon">⏱</span>
      <p><strong>Speed is critical:</strong> AWS CloudWatch logs default to 1-day retention for some services. Auto-scaling events may terminate instances within minutes. Forensic preservation must begin within hours of incident detection, not days.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Phase 3: Collection</h3>
    <p>Cloud evidence collection uses APIs rather than physical imaging tools. Evidence sources include: CloudTrail logs (API calls), VPC Flow Logs (network traffic), S3 access logs, CloudWatch metrics, DNS query logs (Route53), Lambda function logs, container logs (CloudWatch Container Insights), and database query logs (RDS logs, DynamoDB streams).</p>

    <h3>Phase 4: Examination</h3>
    <p>Raw logs must be parsed, filtered, and correlated. A typical AWS CloudTrail log file contains hundreds of thousands of API calls. Examination tools help investigators filter for specific event types (ConsoleLogin, DeleteBucket, CreateUser), correlate events across services, identify unusual source IPs, and reconstruct the attacker's actions step by step.</p>

    <h3>Phase 5: Analysis</h3>
    <p>Analysis reconstructs the full attack timeline: initial access, lateral movement, privilege escalation, data access, and exfiltration. This is where forensic skill meets domain expertise. Analysts correlate CloudTrail logs with VPC Flow Logs with GuardDuty findings to build a coherent narrative of "what the attacker did, when, and how."</p>

    <h3>Phase 6: Reporting</h3>
    <p>Reports must meet two standards simultaneously: technical precision (sufficient for engineers to remediate) and legal admissibility (chain of custody documented, methods described, evidence integrity verified). Cloud forensic reports must document the collection methodology, hash values of all collected artifacts, timestamps in UTC, and the credentials used to access cloud APIs (to demonstrate authority and authorization).</p>
  </section>

