  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <section class="section" id="s7">
    <h2>7. Advantages &amp; Challenges</h2>

    <div class="pro-con">
      <div class="pro-box">
        <h4>✓ Advantages of Cloud Forensics</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Centralized, structured logging from cloud-native services</li>
          <li>No physical evidence handling or chain-of-custody transport</li>
          <li>Scalable log analysis using cloud-native analytics tools</li>
          <li>Immutable audit trails when properly configured</li>
          <li>API-driven automation enables rapid evidence collection</li>
          <li>Global log aggregation across distributed resources</li>
          <li>Built-in redundancy — evidence is rarely truly "lost" if logging enabled</li>
          <li>Provider security certifications provide framework compliance</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="con-box">
        <h4>✗ Challenges &amp; Limitations</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Evidence volatility — VM terminations destroy memory and ephemeral data</li>
          <li>Multi-jurisdiction complexity with conflicting legal frameworks</li>
          <li>Limited access to hypervisor and physical infrastructure layers</li>
          <li>Multi-tenancy prevents isolation of physical evidence</li>
          <li>Log availability depends entirely on customer configuration</li>
          <li>Cloud provider cooperation required for certain evidence types</li>
          <li>Encryption challenges — provider may hold decryption keys</li>
          <li>Rapidly evolving technology outpaces forensic tool development</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

