  <!-- SECTION 8 -->
  <section class="section" id="s8">
    <h2>8. Real-World Case Studies</h2>

    <div class="case-study">
      <div class="case-title">Capital One Data Breach (2019) — AWS Misconfiguration</div>
      <p><strong>What Happened:</strong> A former AWS employee exploited a misconfigured Web Application Firewall at Capital One. Using a Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) attack, she queried the AWS EC2 metadata service, obtained IAM credentials, and used them to exfiltrate over 100 million customer records from S3 buckets.</p>
      <p><strong>The Forensic Investigation:</strong> AWS CloudTrail logs were critical — they recorded every API call made by the attacker's assumed IAM role, including the exact S3 GetObject requests that downloaded customer data. The logs showed precisely which buckets were accessed, what was downloaded, and the timestamps of each action. The attacker was identified because she publicly boasted about the breach on Slack, and investigators correlated her IP addresses with CloudTrail source IPs.</p>
      <p><strong>Shared Responsibility Lesson:</strong> AWS's infrastructure was secure. The misconfiguration was 100% Capital One's responsibility — they had granted the WAF role overly broad S3 read permissions, and they had not enabled GuardDuty anomaly detection. The forensic evidence was available in CloudTrail; the failure was in prevention, not detection.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <div class="case-title">Solar Winds Orion Attack (2020) — Supply Chain via Cloud</div>
      <p><strong>What Happened:</strong> Russian state-sponsored hackers (APT29/Cozy Bear) compromised SolarWinds' software build pipeline, inserting a backdoor ("SUNBURST") into legitimate Orion software updates. The backdoor was distributed to 18,000+ organizations and used to pivot into cloud environments, particularly Microsoft 365 and Azure Active Directory.</p>
      <p><strong>The Forensic Investigation:</strong> Investigators discovered that once inside victim environments, the attackers forged SAML authentication tokens, granting themselves access to cloud resources without valid credentials. Microsoft's forensic team analyzed Azure AD sign-in logs and found authentication events with forged tokens — identifiable because they used anomalous certificate issuers. The attacker's lateral movement through M365 was reconstructed entirely from the Unified Audit Log.</p>
      <p><strong>Shared Responsibility Lesson:</strong> Even with perfect customer security configuration, supply chain attacks can bypass all controls. This case accelerated the adoption of Zero Trust architecture, where every access request is verified regardless of network origin.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <div class="case-title">Twitch Data Breach (2021) — Insider Access &amp; Git Leak</div>
      <p><strong>What Happened:</strong> An anonymous attacker obtained and leaked 125GB of Twitch's internal data, including streamer payout information, proprietary source code, and internal tools. The breach originated from an improperly secured internal Git repository accessible via the cloud.</p>
      <p><strong>The Cloud Forensic Challenge:</strong> When source code repositories and internal documentation are hosted on cloud-based Git services, access logs become the primary forensic evidence source. Twitch's cloud logs had to be analyzed to determine which API tokens were used for the exfiltration, when the data was cloned, and from which IP addresses — a classic cloud forensic log reconstruction exercise.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

