  <!-- SECTION 9 -->
  <section class="section" id="s9">
    <h2>9. Tools &amp; Technologies</h2>

    <div class="cards-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">📋</span>
        <h4>AWS CloudTrail</h4>
        <p>Records all AWS API calls with user identity, timestamp, source IP, and parameters. Primary evidence source for AWS incident response.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🌊</span>
        <h4>VPC Flow Logs</h4>
        <p>Captures IP traffic metadata (not payloads) for network interfaces in AWS VPCs. Essential for detecting lateral movement and data exfiltration.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🔍</span>
        <h4>AWS GuardDuty</h4>
        <p>ML-powered threat detection analyzing CloudTrail, DNS, and VPC logs for anomalies. Automatically alerts on credential misuse and unusual API patterns.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">📊</span>
        <h4>Azure Sentinel</h4>
        <p>Cloud-native SIEM and SOAR platform. Aggregates logs from Azure and third-party sources for correlation, threat hunting, and automated incident response.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🏗</span>
        <h4>GCP Chronicle</h4>
        <p>Google's cloud-native security analytics platform, providing petabyte-scale log retention and YARA-L threat hunting rules.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">💾</span>
        <h4>Volatility Framework</h4>
        <p>Open-source memory forensics framework adapted for cloud VM memory analysis. Can analyze memory dumps from cloud VMs for process artifacts.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🕸</span>
        <h4>Magnet AXIOM Cyber</h4>
        <p>Commercial forensic platform with cloud acquisition capabilities for AWS, Azure, GCP, Microsoft 365, and Google Workspace evidence collection.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🔬</span>
        <h4>DFIR.cloud</h4>
        <p>Open-source collection of cloud forensic scripts and tools specifically designed for cloud incident response workflows.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Key Evidence Types in Cloud Investigations</h3>
    <ul class="styled">
      <li><strong>CloudTrail / Audit Logs:</strong> Records of every management-plane API call — who did what, when, from where</li>
      <li><strong>Access Logs (S3, GCS, Blob):</strong> Object-level access records for cloud storage — every file read, written, or deleted</li>
      <li><strong>Authentication Logs:</strong> Sign-in attempts, MFA events, SAML assertions, OAuth token grants</li>
      <li><strong>Network Flow Logs:</strong> Layer-3 traffic metadata for detecting C2 communication and data exfiltration</li>
      <li><strong>VM Snapshots:</strong> Point-in-time disk images containing OS artifacts, user files, and installed software</li>
      <li><strong>Container Logs:</strong> stdout/stderr output from containerized workloads, Kubernetes audit logs</li>
      <li><strong>Serverless Execution Logs:</strong> Lambda / Cloud Functions execution records with input/output and duration</li>
      <li><strong>Database Audit Logs:</strong> Queries executed against RDS, DynamoDB, BigQuery — critical for data theft investigations</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

